## Overdue dates

`Highlighting.overdueDate(diff)` returns a fixed class from the difference to today. These use Primer's semantic foreground colors and have nothing to do with resource colors. `__hl_date_not_overdue` is a marker with no styling attached.

<%= embed Colors::HighlightingPreview, :overdue_dates %>

## Themes

The generated stylesheet is identical for every user; it holds colors, not decisions. Light, dark and both high contrast variants are resolved in CSS against the `data-color-mode` and `data-light-theme`/`data-dark-theme` attributes that the theme controller stamps onto `<body>`.

Light is the default and dark an override, because those attributes only appear once Stimulus connects. A rule that must apply in light mode belongs on the bare class, not under a `[data-color-mode="light"]` selector.

Derived values stay in CSS on purpose:

```sass
--hl-lightness-switch: max(0, min(calc(1 / (0.453 - var(--hl-perceived-lightness))), 1))
--hl-lighten-by: calc((0.6 - var(--hl-perceived-lightness)) * 100 * var(--hl-lightness-switch))
```

`--hl-lightness-switch` collapses to 0 or 1 and is what decides the text color on `__hl_background` in light mode: below a perceived lightness of 0.453 white, above it black. `--hl-lighten-by` is the dark mode counterpart, lightening the color by however far it sits below 0.6.

Saturated blues are where the switch looks most surprising, because they read as darker than their luminance is. `indigo-5` (`#5C7CFA`, 0.4953) lands just above the threshold and gets black text, which is the accessible choice even though it looks odd — 5.72:1 against black versus 3.67:1 against white. Being a single hard cut rather than a contrast calculation, it is not always the better of the two: `indigo-6` (`#4C6EF5`, 0.4412) falls just below and takes white at 4.32:1, where black would have given 4.86:1.

The lightness arithmetic uses relative color syntax, so a single hexcode covers what previously needed separate RGB and HSL triples:

```sass
background: var(--hl-color)
background: rgb(from var(--hl-color) r g b / 0.18)
border: 1px solid hsl(from var(--hl-color) h s calc(l - 15))
```

In `hsl(from …)` the `s` and `l` channels resolve as 0..100 numbers, so `calc(l - 15)` subtracts 15 percentage points of lightness.

## Adding a highlightable resource

1. Register it in `app/views/highlighting/styles.css.erb`. The scope needs to respond to `id` and `color`; `Color` records are accepted as their own color, and plain `Struct`/`Data` constants work too, which is how `Meetings::Statuses::AVAILABLE` is handled.
2. Add the model to `highlight_css_updated_at` in `HighlightingHelper` so the version tag changes when it does. Skipping this serves stale CSS for up to a year.
3. Use the helpers at the call sites. Nothing new is needed in `_colors.sass` unless the resource wants a treatment that does not exist yet.
4. If the color reads as text and the resource can arrive through `inlineClass`, add it to `FOREGROUND_PROPERTIES`.
